 ## Subtopics
 
+### [GaussianFlow](6149-gaussianflow.md)
+GaussianFlow is a point-based differentiable rendering approach for dynamic 3D scene reconstruction that models a 4D scene as a set of deformable 3D Gaussian particles advected by an explicit 3D velocity field. It solves the problem of efficient dynamic scene reconstruction by introducing a Dual-Domain Deformation Model (DDDM) that explicitly models attribute deformations in both time and frequency domains, eliminating the need for implicit neural fields or per-frame optimization. Unlike [Dynamic 3D Gaussian Splatting](5703-dynamic-3d-gaussian-splatting.md) which tracks motion via persistent view synthesis and local constraints, GaussianFlow uses a global flow-based deformation model, achieving 5× faster training than per-frame 3DGS while preserving real-time rendering speeds. Choose it when global flow-based deformation is preferred over local tracking.
+
+### [Dynamic 3D Gaussians](7336-dynamic-3d-gaussians.md)
+Dynamic 3D Gaussians is a method for persistent dynamic view synthesis that models time-varying scenes by linking the motion of each Gaussian primitive to the movement of corresponding 3D points in the scene. It solves the problem of reconstructing and rendering dynamic 3D content from multi-view video by extending static 3D Gaussian Splatting with temporal tracking and deformation constraints. It differs from its parent [Dynamic 3D Gaussian Splatting](5703-dynamic-3d-gaussian-splatting.md) by explicitly defining the tracking mechanism (linking Gaussians to 3D point motion) and from [GaussianFlow](6149-gaussianflow.md) by its reliance on persistent synthesis rather than flow-based deformation fields. Choose it when explicit point-to-Gaussian tracking is required.
+
 ### SpeeDe3DGS
-SpeeDe3DGS accelerates deformable 3DGS by applying temporal pruning [S2, unofficial]. It identifies static and active Gaussians and groups the dynamic parts by motion similarity to reduce the number of deformation queries [S2, unofficial]. This optimization reduces computational overhead without significantly compromising rendering quality.
+SpeeDe3DGS accelerates deformable 3DGS by applying temporal pruning [S2, unofficial]. It identifies static and active Gaussians and groups the dynamic parts by motion similarity to reduce the number of deformation queries [S2, unofficial]. Choose it when computational efficiency for deformable scenes is prioritized over maximum deformation accuracy.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering is $O(N)$ per frame, where $N$ is the number of Gaussians. Optimization time is higher than static 3DGS due to the additional temporal constraints and tracking calculations.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$, storing only canonical parameters and potentially small deformation fields or constraints. This is significantly more compact than per-frame baselines.
-*   **Performance:** Enables real-time rendering of dynamic scenes. The specific frame rates depend on the hardware and scene complexity, but the method is designed for real-time applicability.
-*   **Strengths:** Persistent representation avoids storage explosion; temporal coherence reduces flicker; local-rigid constraints preserve geometry.
-*   **Limitations:** May struggle with highly non-rigid deformations that violate the ARAP/isometry assumptions; requires careful tuning of temporal loss weights.
